            <div xml:id="d611">

                <pb facs="0780" n="668" sameAs="frus:frus1918/pg668"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/21613</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Summerlin</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-05T18:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 5, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>685. See Department’s 681, January 4, 8 p.m. You might suggest as a solution of
                    the grave Yaqui situation that Mexican Government <pb facs="0781" n="669"
                        xml:id="pg669"/> immediately send force of approximately 5,000 troops to
                    Sonora to restore order and garrison danger points. You may intimate that you
                    can secure passage over American territory for such an expedition.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
